{"belief_text": "Goal: Create /app/data.comp (max 2500 bytes) such that 'cat data.comp | /app/decomp' outputs exactly the content of /app/data.txt. The decompressor /app/decomp.c implements arithmetic coding with LZ77 back-references: radix=255, range initialized to 1, LITSIZE=4, contexts for operations (1), back-references (2-3 with OFF1=5, OFF2=2), and literals (8-9). The decoder subtracts 1 from each input byte (gc()-1), uses get_bit with split=range*(counts[0]+1)/(counts[0]+counts[1]+2), and get_integer with subtract_it=(1<<tmp) encoding. Data.txt contains 4868 bytes of text about data compression.

Three encoder implementations exist: encoder.pl (5429 bytes Perl, successfully generates only 61 bytes output but writes plaintext status message instead of binary data to file despite correct binmode file output at lines 197-199), encoder.c (3469 bytes C with range=1 initialization, clamped (low/RADIX)+1 output in normalize and flush, produces 4099 bytes causing stack-smashing at 87 bytes decoded), and minimal_encoder.pl (2066 bytes, produces 35 bytes for 'Hi' test but decompresses to 0 bytes). Perl and gcc are available.

Unresolved gaps: Why encoder.pl normalize produces bytes exceeding 255 (303, 279, 415) when radix=255 despite modulo operations, corrupting pack('C*') output. How to correct arithmetic in encoder.pl to bound output to 0-255 range. Current data.comp from C encoder is 4099 bytes (exceeds limit by 1599 bytes) and causes stack corruption. Need working encoder producing valid compressed stream under 2500 bytes that decompresses correctly."}

